06 – Database Design and Data Model
1. Document Purpose

This document defines the database design and data model for the B2B Order Management Platform.

It describes the main database entities, attributes, relationships, constraints, and data management approach required to support users, products, inventory, carts, orders, invoices, and audit activities.

The database will use PostgreSQL as the primary relational database management system.

2. Database Objectives

The database design aims to:

Store application data in a structured format.
Maintain relationships between related business entities.
Prevent invalid or inconsistent data.
Support customer and administrator workflows.
Maintain order and inventory consistency.
Support authentication and authorization.
Store invoice information.
Maintain audit activity records.
Support efficient data retrieval.
Provide a foundation for future system expansion.
3. Database Technology

Database Management System: PostgreSQL

PostgreSQL is selected because it provides:

Relational data management
Primary and foreign key constraints
Transaction support
Data integrity
Structured query capabilities
Support for scalable application development
4. Main Database Entities

The initial database will contain the following major entities:

Users
Products
Carts
Cart Items
Orders
Order Items
Invoices
Audit Activities
5. Entity Relationship Overview

The main relationships are:

Users
  │
  ├───────────────┐
  │               │
  ▼               ▼
Carts            Orders
  │               │
  ▼               ├──────────────┐
Cart Items        │              │
  │               ▼              ▼
  ▼          Order Items       Invoices
Products
  ▲
  │
  │
Inventory is maintained through product stock information

Users
  │
  ▼
Audit Activities

6. Users Table
Table Name

users

Purpose

Stores customer and administrator account information.

Columns

| Column        | Data Type    | Constraints      | Description            |
| ------------- | ------------ | ---------------- | ---------------------- |
| id            | BIGSERIAL    | PRIMARY KEY      | Unique user identifier |
| name          | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL         | User's full name       |
| company_name  | VARCHAR(150) | NOT NULL         | Customer company name  |
| email         | VARCHAR(150) | UNIQUE, NOT NULL | User email address     |
| phone         | VARCHAR(20)  | NOT NULL         | User phone number      |
| password_hash | TEXT         | NOT NULL         | Hashed password        |
| role          | VARCHAR(20)  | NOT NULL         | User role              |
| created_at    | TIMESTAMP    | NOT NULL         | Account creation time  |
| updated_at    | TIMESTAMP    | NOT NULL         | Last update time       |


Role Values

The initial supported roles are:

customer
admin

7. Products Table
Table Name

products

Purpose

Stores information about products available for purchase.

Columns

| Column         | Data Type     | Constraints | Description                 |
| -------------- | ------------- | ----------- | --------------------------- |
| id             | BIGSERIAL     | PRIMARY KEY | Unique product identifier   |
| name           | VARCHAR(150)  | NOT NULL    | Product name                |
| description    | TEXT          | NULL        | Product description         |
| price          | NUMERIC(12,2) | NOT NULL    | Product price               |
| stock_quantity | INTEGER       | NOT NULL    | Available stock             |
| status         | VARCHAR(20)   | NOT NULL    | Product availability status |
| created_at     | TIMESTAMP     | NOT NULL    | Product creation time       |
| updated_at     | TIMESTAMP     | NOT NULL    | Last update time            |


8. Carts Table
Table Name

carts

Purpose

Stores the shopping cart associated with a customer.

Columns

| Column     | Data Type | Constraints                   | Description                       |
| ---------- | --------- | ----------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| id         | BIGSERIAL | PRIMARY KEY                   | Unique cart identifier            |
| user_id    | BIGINT    | FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL, UNIQUE | Customer associated with the cart |
| created_at | TIMESTAMP | NOT NULL                      | Cart creation time                |
| updated_at | TIMESTAMP | NOT NULL                      | Last cart update time             |


Relationship

One user can have one active cart.

users 1 ───────── 1 carts

9. Cart Items Table
Table Name

cart_items

Purpose

Stores products and quantities added to customer carts.

Columns

| Column     | Data Type | Constraints           | Description                 |
| ---------- | --------- | --------------------- | --------------------------- |
| id         | BIGSERIAL | PRIMARY KEY           | Unique cart item identifier |
| cart_id    | BIGINT    | FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL | Associated cart             |
| product_id | BIGINT    | FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL | Associated product          |
| quantity   | INTEGER   | NOT NULL              | Requested quantity          |


carts 1 ───────── many cart_items

products 1 ───── many cart_items

A unique constraint on (cart_id, product_id) should be considered to prevent duplicate entries for the same product in one cart.

10. Orders Table
Table Name

orders

Purpose

Stores customer order information.

Columns

| Column       | Data Type     | Constraints           | Description                   |
| ------------ | ------------- | --------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| id           | BIGSERIAL     | PRIMARY KEY           | Unique order identifier       |
| user_id      | BIGINT        | FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL | Customer who placed the order |
| total_amount | NUMERIC(12,2) | NOT NULL              | Total order amount            |
| status       | VARCHAR(30)   | NOT NULL              | Current order status          |
| created_at   | TIMESTAMP     | NOT NULL              | Order creation time           |
| updated_at   | TIMESTAMP     | NOT NULL              | Last order update time        |


Possible Order Status Values
pending
confirmed
processing
shipped
delivered
cancelled

The exact workflow may be adjusted during backend implementation.

Relationship
users 1 ───────── many orders
11. Order Items Table
Table Name

order_items

Purpose

Stores individual products included in an order.

Columns

| Column     | Data Type     | Constraints           | Description                       |
| ---------- | ------------- | --------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| id         | BIGSERIAL     | PRIMARY KEY           | Unique order item identifier      |
| order_id   | BIGINT        | FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL | Associated order                  |
| product_id | BIGINT        | FOREIGN KEY, NOT NULL | Ordered product                   |
| quantity   | INTEGER       | NOT NULL              | Ordered quantity                  |
| unit_price | NUMERIC(12,2) | NOT NULL              | Product price at order time       |
| subtotal   | NUMERIC(12,2) | NOT NULL              | Quantity multiplied by unit price |



Relationships
orders 1 ───────── many order_items

products 1 ────── many order_items
Important Design Decision

unit_price is stored in order_items instead of relying only on the current product price.

This preserves the price that was applicable when the order was placed, even if the product price changes later.

12. Invoices Table
Table Name

invoices

Purpose

Stores invoice information associated with an order.

Columns

| Column         | Data Type     | Constraints                   | Description               |
| -------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------- |
| id             | BIGSERIAL     | PRIMARY KEY                   | Unique invoice identifier |
| order_id       | BIGINT        | FOREIGN KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL | Associated order          |
| invoice_number | VARCHAR(50)   | UNIQUE, NOT NULL              | Unique invoice number     |
| total_amount   | NUMERIC(12,2) | NOT NULL                      | Invoice total             |
| generated_at   | TIMESTAMP     | NOT NULL                      | Invoice generation time   |


Relationship
orders 1 ───────── 1 invoices

One order is associated with one invoice in the initial system design.

13. Audit Activities Table
Table Name

audit_activities

Purpose

Stores important user and administrator activities for traceability.

Columns

| Column      | Data Type    | Constraints       | Description                   |
| ----------- | ------------ | ----------------- | ----------------------------- |
| id          | BIGSERIAL    | PRIMARY KEY       | Unique activity identifier    |
| user_id     | BIGINT       | FOREIGN KEY, NULL | User who performed the action |
| action      | VARCHAR(100) | NOT NULL          | Action performed              |
| entity_type | VARCHAR(50)  | NULL              | Type of affected entity       |
| entity_id   | BIGINT       | NULL              | ID of affected entity         |
| description | TEXT         | NULL              | Activity description          |
| created_at  | TIMESTAMP    | NOT NULL          | Activity time                 |

Examples of Activities
Product created
Product updated
Product deleted
Inventory updated
Order status updated
User login
Administrative action performed
14. Primary Key Design

Every major table will have a unique primary key.

Primary keys:

users.id
products.id
carts.id
cart_items.id
orders.id
order_items.id
invoices.id
audit_activities.id

Primary keys ensure that each record can be uniquely identified.

15. Foreign Key Relationships

Foreign keys will maintain relationships between related tables.

| Table            | Foreign Key | References  |
| ---------------- | ----------- | ----------- |
| carts            | user_id     | users.id    |
| cart_items       | cart_id     | carts.id    |
| cart_items       | product_id  | products.id |
| orders           | user_id     | users.id    |
| order_items      | order_id    | orders.id   |
| order_items      | product_id  | products.id |
| invoices         | order_id    | orders.id   |
| audit_activities | user_id     | users.id    |


16. Relationship Summary

The main relationships are:

User
 │
 ├── 1 : 1 ── Cart
 │              │
 │              └── 1 : Many ── Cart Items ── Many : 1 ── Product
 │
 ├── 1 : Many ── Orders
 │                  │
 │                  ├── 1 : Many ── Order Items ── Many : 1 ── Product
 │                  │
 │                  └── 1 : 1 ── Invoice
 │
 └── 1 : Many ── Audit Activities

 17. Data Integrity Rules

The database should enforce important integrity rules.

Examples include:

User email must be unique.
User name cannot be empty.
Product name cannot be empty.
Product price must be greater than or equal to zero.
Product stock quantity cannot be negative.
Cart quantity must be greater than zero.
Order quantity must be greater than zero.
Order total must be greater than or equal to zero.
Invoice number must be unique.
Required foreign key relationships must reference existing records.
18. Inventory Rules

Inventory data must remain consistent with order processing.

The following rules apply:

Stock quantity cannot be negative.
Customers cannot place an order exceeding available stock.
Stock should be reduced after a successful order.
Failed order operations should not incorrectly reduce stock.
Administrators can update stock through authorized functionality.
Inventory updates should be recorded when appropriate.
19. Order Data Rules

The order system should follow these rules:

Every order must belong to a valid customer.
Every order must contain at least one order item.
Every order item must reference a valid product.
Quantity must be greater than zero.
Unit price must be stored at the time of ordering.
Order total should be calculated from order items.
Order status must contain a valid status value.
Order information should not be accessible to unauthorized customers.
20. Database Transaction Requirements

Certain operations involve multiple database changes and should be handled using database transactions.

For example, order creation may require:

Begin Transaction
      ↓
Validate Product Stock
      ↓
Create Order
      ↓
Create Order Items
      ↓
Update Product Stock
      ↓
Create Invoice Information
      ↓
Record Audit Activity
      ↓
Commit Transaction
If a critical operation fails, the transaction should be rolled back where appropriate to prevent inconsistent data.

21. Database Migration Strategy

Database schema changes will be maintained through migration files.

Migrations may be used for:

Creating tables
Adding columns
Modifying constraints
Creating indexes
Updating database structure

Each migration should have a clear and descriptive name.

Example:

001-create-users-table
002-create-products-table
003-create-carts-table
004-create-cart-items-table
005-create-orders-table
006-create-order-items-table
007-create-invoices-table
008-create-audit-activities-table

The exact migration tool will be selected during backend implementation.

22. Indexing Considerations

Indexes may be created for frequently searched or joined fields.

Potential indexes include:

users.email
products.name
orders.user_id
orders.status
order_items.order_id
cart_items.cart_id
audit_activities.user_id
audit_activities.created_at

Indexes will be added based on application requirements and observed query usage.

23. Data Security

The following database security practices will be followed:

Passwords will be stored only as secure hashes.
Database credentials will not be hard-coded into application source code.
Sensitive configuration will use environment variables.
Database access will be restricted to the backend application.
SQL operations will use safe parameterized queries or an appropriate database abstraction layer.
Unauthorized users will not receive direct database access.
Development data will be controlled and authorized.
24. Data Lifecycle

The general data lifecycle is:

User Input
    ↓
Frontend Validation
    ↓
Backend Validation
    ↓
Business Logic
    ↓
Database Operation
    ↓
Data Stored / Updated
    ↓
API Response
    ↓
Frontend Display


For orders:

Product Selection
    ↓
Cart
    ↓
Order Creation
    ↓
Inventory Update
    ↓
Invoice
    ↓
Order History
    ↓
Audit Activity
25. Database Backup Considerations

During local development, database backups may be created when required.

For future production deployment, a proper backup strategy should include:

Regular automated backups
Backup retention
Recovery procedures
Secure backup storage
Periodic restoration testing

Production backup configuration is outside the initial internship implementation scope.

26. Future Database Enhancements

Potential future enhancements include:

Product categories
Multiple product images
Suppliers
Addresses
Payment records
Shipment tracking
Notifications
Discount and coupon management
Purchase history analytics
Advanced reporting
Soft-delete support
Database performance optimization
27. Data Model Summary

The database design provides a relational structure for the complete B2B order workflow.

The users table manages system users, while products stores product and inventory information.

carts and cart_items support customer cart management.

orders and order_items store customer purchases, while invoices maintain invoice information.

audit_activities provides traceability for important system actions.

Foreign keys, constraints, validation rules, and transactions will help maintain data consistency throughout the application.

